<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen W. Kearny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1794–1848</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen W. Kearny was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army officer who commanded the Army of the West in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>. In August 1846 he occupied Santa Fe without a battle and proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> American territory before marching on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, where he fought at San Pasqual and served briefly as military governor. He died in 1848 after further service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-w-kearny.jpg" alt="Stephen W. Kearny" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Engraved by T. B. Welch, expressly for Graham's Magazine, July 1849. Peter A. Juley &amp;amp; Son, photographers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stephen_W._Kearny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
